WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.330
Step away from a fire and you get colder.

2
00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:11.300
Step away from the Sun's surface and the temperature jumps a hundredfold — from five thousand five hundred degrees to over a million.

3
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:18.310
For eighty years physics has been unable to explain why, and a probe had to fly into that heat to look for the answer.

4
00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:30.320
The farther you stand from a fire, the colder you get.

5
00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:38.610
On the Sun, the opposite happens: as you climb away from the surface, the temperature, instead of falling, rises a hundredfold.

6
00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:40.980
Physics does not forbid this.

7
00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:46.230
But for a hundred and eighty years, nobody has been able to say for certain how it happens.

8
00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:49.720
August seventh, eighteen sixty-nine.

9
00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:58.010
In the American Midwest, in the field-scented little towns of Iowa, a handful of people have turned their telescopes to the sky and are holding their breath.

10
00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:03.420
A total solar eclipse is about to cut across the continent from one edge to the other.

11
00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:07.550
The observers are carrying a new instrument: the spectroscope.

12
00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:16.320
It passes light through a prism, splits it into its colours, and from the thin lines hidden inside those colours it lets you read off the identity of matter.

13
00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.770
In those years, the device was rewriting astronomy from the ground up.

14
00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:25.380
For the first time, you could say what a star was made of without ever touching it.

15
00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:35.590
The moment the Moon covers the Sun's brilliant disc completely, the sky sinks into twilight and something appears: a pearl-white, silken, ragged crown wrapped around the Sun.

16
00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:38.440
The corona.

17
00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:44.970
Under ordinary conditions it is invisible, because the surface of the Sun drowns it in light millions of times brighter.

18
00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:49.100
Only during those few minutes of totality does it show itself.

19
00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:52.590
In Burlington, Charles Augustus Young.

20
00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:55.760
In Des Moines, William Harkness.

21
00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:00.690
Independently of each other, they turned their instruments on the spectrum of the corona.

22
00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:05.620
And both saw the same thing: a bright, sharp, vivid green line.

23
00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:09.590
Its wavelength, five hundred thirty point three nanometres.

24
00:02:09.590 --> 00:02:18.840
Because it fell at number fourteen seventy-four on the scale Kirchhoff was using at the time, it was known for years simply as the fourteen seventy-four line.

25
00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:21.370
The trouble was this.

26
00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:24.220
That line belonged to nothing.

27
00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:28.830
No element known in any laboratory on Earth had such a line in its spectrum.

28
00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:34.720
Looking back from today, the first reaction that comes to mind would be that it must be a measurement error.

29
00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:43.650
But in eighteen sixty-nine there was no reason to think so, because exactly one year earlier the same thing had happened and had ended in a magnificent triumph.

30
00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:54.980
During the eclipse of eighteen sixty-eight, Janssen in India and Lockyer in London had each seen an unrecognised line at the Sun's edge, had attributed it to a new element, and had named it helium,

31
00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:57.350
from the Greek root for sun.

32
00:02:57.350 --> 00:03:01.480
Helium would not be found on Earth for another twenty-seven years.

33
00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:07.210
So there it was: an element discovered in the sky and only afterwards confirmed underfoot.

34
00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:10.220
The recipe had already worked once.

35
00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:14.190
And so the green line was given a name of its own: coronium.

36
00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:16.240
The corona element.

37
00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:21.490
A mysterious substance present only in the Sun's crown and encountered nowhere on Earth.

38
00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:32.340
Even Mendeleev, the architect of the periodic table, at one point seriously entertained clearing a place for coronium at the very head of the table, reasoning that it had to be lighter than hydrogen.

39
00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:36.150
Coronium waited seventy years for a seat that never came.

40
00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:44.920
In the time that passed, quantum mechanics was born, the inner architecture of the atom was worked out, and it became clear how electron shells emit light.

41
00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:50.010
And in the periodic table, no gap was left into which coronium could be fitted.

42
00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:55.580
However hard anyone looked, there was no room for a new element behind that green line.

43
00:03:55.580 --> 00:04:02.590
The answer arrived in nineteen thirty-nine, from the German astrophysicist Walter Grotrian, working at Potsdam.

44
00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:08.480
Grotrian realised that the corona's red line was not a new element at all, but ordinary iron.

45
00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:14.370
Except not ordinary in the least: iron that had lost nine of its electrons.

46
00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:21.220
Within a few years the Swedish spectroscopist Bengt Edlén finished the job and identified that famous green line too.

47
00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:23.590
The source was iron again.

48
00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:27.080
This time, iron stripped of thirteen electrons.

49
00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:30.570
This looks like the solution to a chemistry puzzle.

50
00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:32.780
It is not.

51
00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:36.270
It is the sentence that opens the door to a far larger one.

52
00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:40.880
Because tearing thirteen electrons off an iron atom is no small matter.

53
00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:45.810
For that to happen, atoms have to collide with a violence that is hard to imagine.

54
00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:49.460
The temperature required is around two million degrees.

55
00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:52.310
Now let us make the comparison.

56
00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:59.000
The surface of the Sun that we see with our eyes, the photosphere, is about five thousand five hundred degrees.

57
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:03.610
The core, where the energy is produced, is fifteen million.

58
00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:06.940
So the heat source sits at the very centre.

59
00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:14.750
As you move outward from the centre, the temperature falls, and falls, and drops to five thousand five hundred at the surface.

60
00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:21.600
Up to this point everything agrees with intuition: the farther you move from the fire, the cooler you get.

61
00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:24.610
Then the strange thing happens.

62
00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:34.180
Just above the surface, in the layer called the chromosphere, the temperature first dips a little further, down to around four thousand degrees, and then begins to climb.

63
00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:37.190
It rises into the tens of thousands.

64
00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:45.640
And then, in a wafer-thin layer called the transition region, no more than a few hundred kilometres thick, the temperature explodes.

65
00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:49.770
It leaps from hundreds of thousands into the millions.

66
00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:54.700
By the time you reach the corona, you are standing in one to three million degrees.

67
00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:58.190
You are moving away from the fire and getting hotter.

68
00:05:58.190 --> 00:06:02.160
You step backward from the campfire and start to burn.

69
00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:08.370
Let us close off one misunderstanding right away, because the direction of the astonishment matters.

70
00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:13.460
The corona is millions of degrees, but it is also unbelievably thin.

71
00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:19.190
The matter inside it is so sparse that it competes with the best vacuums we can make in a laboratory.

72
00:06:19.190 --> 00:06:24.280
Temperature is a measure of the average speed of particles, not of the total quantity of heat.

73
00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:32.890
Sparks from a sparkler are a thousand five hundred degrees, yet they do not burn your hand, because each spark contains almost no matter at all.

74
00:06:32.890 --> 00:06:35.740
The corona is like that.

75
00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:39.550
So the problem is not how the corona carries so much energy.

76
00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:44.960
The problem is far sharper than that: how does the energy get up there in the first place?

77
00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:48.930
Because heat does not flow from cold to hot on its own.

78
00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:53.220
That is one of the most fundamental laws of thermodynamics.

79
00:06:53.220 --> 00:07:01.030
A surface at five thousand five hundred degrees cannot heat a gas at two million degrees above it, not by radiation and not by conduction.

80
00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:04.840
You cannot boil water by putting it in the refrigerator.

81
00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:09.290
Which means the energy reaching the corona does not travel there as heat.

82
00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:14.700
It travels in some other guise, a guise that only turns into heat once it arrives.

83
00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:21.550
And what that guise is has been, for a century and a half, one of astrophysics' most stubborn open questions.

84
00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:29.040
Let us begin the search for the culprit the way any investigation begins: by working out how much energy is actually required.

85
00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:33.490
How much power does it take to hold the corona at millions of degrees?

86
00:07:33.490 --> 00:07:36.180
Surprisingly little.

87
00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:40.790
In quiet regions a few hundred watts per square metre is enough.

88
00:07:40.790 --> 00:07:47.640
In the active regions, where magnetic activity is concentrated, the figure climbs to something like ten kilowatts.

89
00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:59.130
Set against the total energy streaming off the Sun's surface, that amounts to a few parts in a million for the quiet corona, and even in the most turbulent places only about one part in ten thousand.

90
00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:04.700
In other words, the Sun does this job while setting aside almost nothing from its budget.

91
00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:07.870
The problem is not a shortage of energy.

92
00:08:07.870 --> 00:08:12.960
The problem is delivering that energy to the right place, in the right form.

93
00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:17.730
On the question of where the energy originates, there is no serious argument.

94
00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:22.980
Beneath the Sun's surface lies the convection zone: an enormous boiling soup.

95
00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:28.390
Hot plasma rises, reaches the surface, cools, and sinks.

96
00:08:28.390 --> 00:08:35.080
At the surface this appears to us as a simmering texture of cells called granules, each one roughly the size of Turkey.

97
00:08:35.080 --> 00:08:39.210
And this plasma drags the magnetic field lines along with it.

98
00:08:39.210 --> 00:08:44.940
The footpoints of those field lines are shoved, jostled, twisted and tangled without pause.

99
00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:51.470
That is the corona's energy source: the boiling beneath the surface, pumped upward through the magnetic field.

100
00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:54.000
The fight breaks out after that.

101
00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:58.770
How does this energy travel up, and how does it turn into heat once it is there?

102
00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:04.340
The first suspect is the idea put forward by Eugene Parker: nanoflares.

103
00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:07.190
Parker's reasoning runs like this.

104
00:09:07.190 --> 00:09:15.640
Because their footpoints are in constant random motion at the surface, the magnetic field lines in the corona become steadily more tangled with one another.

105
00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:20.250
Rather like the headphone cable in your pocket knotting itself.

106
00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:23.260
That tangling stores tension in the field.

107
00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:28.190
But magnetic field lines inside a plasma cannot endure unlimited twisting.

108
00:09:28.190 --> 00:09:35.520
At some point a critical threshold is crossed, the lines snap and reconnect, and the stored tension is released all at once.

109
00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:38.850
This is called magnetic reconnection.

110
00:09:38.850 --> 00:09:46.340
Parker calculated that such events would carry about a billionth of the energy of a large solar flare, and he named them nanoflares.

111
00:09:46.340 --> 00:09:48.870
One alone is negligible.

112
00:09:48.870 --> 00:09:57.000
But if the corona is crackling with these tiny detonations everywhere, all the time, the sum of them is enough to account for millions of degrees.

113
00:09:57.000 --> 00:10:00.170
The second suspect is waves.

114
00:10:00.170 --> 00:10:10.700
The root of this idea reaches back to the Swedish physicist Hannes Alfvén, who was awarded the Nobel Prize for work showing that plasma threaded by a magnetic field can behave like a stretched wire,

115
00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:13.710
which is to say that it can carry waves.

116
00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:19.760
Picture a guitar string, except this string is made of plasma and held taut by the magnetic field.

117
00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:27.410
The boiling at the surface plucks and shakes that string constantly, and the vibration travels upward as a wave, into the corona.

118
00:10:27.410 --> 00:10:32.180
Energy is carried that way quietly, without any explosion at all.

119
00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:35.030
The trouble with waves is not the carrying.

120
00:10:35.030 --> 00:10:37.080
It is the letting go.

121
00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:40.890
By their nature these waves are extremely resistant to damping.

122
00:10:40.890 --> 00:10:46.460
Instead of depositing their energy in the corona, they tend to pick it up and haul it off into space.

123
00:10:46.460 --> 00:10:51.390
So the hard part of the theory is reining the waves in at exactly the right place.

124
00:10:51.390 --> 00:11:03.040
There are proposed mechanisms: waves colliding with each other and collapsing into turbulence; neighbouring magnetic tubes oscillating at different rates and shearing against one another on microscopic scales;

125
00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:08.450
energy cascading into ever smaller structures until it finally becomes particle motion.

126
00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:10.820
Each one works mathematically.

127
00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:15.270
Which of them dominates on the real Sun is another question entirely.

128
00:11:15.270 --> 00:11:20.040
So why can we not simply tell the two suspects apart?

129
00:11:20.040 --> 00:11:26.570
Because both produce more or less the same outcome: a hot, churning corona at millions of degrees.

130
00:11:26.570 --> 00:11:32.140
There are distinguishing signatures, but every one of them sits right at the edge of our resolution.

131
00:11:32.140 --> 00:11:37.870
If nanoflares are the answer, the heating should be impulsive: sudden, brief, local.

132
00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:45.680
It should leave behind tiny pockets of plasma that briefly exceed ten million degrees, and non-thermal accelerated particles.

133
00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:53.170
If waves are the answer, the heating should be smoother and more continuous, and the swaying of the magnetic tubes should be directly visible.

134
00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:56.180
Both sides have found their evidence.

135
00:11:56.180 --> 00:12:07.510
The Japanese solar observatory Hinode, along with ground-based spectrometers, genuinely demonstrated the presence of waves in the corona and the chromosphere; magnetic tubes were recorded swaying,

136
00:12:07.510 --> 00:12:10.200
spicules were recorded trembling.

137
00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:20.090
On the other hand, telescopes sensitive to hard X-rays caught traces of unexpectedly hot plasma in regions that looked perfectly quiet, and that is the fingerprint of impulsive heating.

138
00:12:20.090 --> 00:12:31.100
The European Space Agency's Solar Orbiter, which began operating in twenty twenty, showed in its very first images countless tiny flashes scattered across the whole face of the corona,

139
00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:35.070
ranging from a few hundred to a few thousand kilometres across.

140
00:12:35.070 --> 00:12:37.440
They were christened campfires.

141
00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:41.250
They looked like the visual counterpart of the nanoflare idea.

142
00:12:41.250 --> 00:12:50.660
Yet the measurements revealed that most of these fires occurred higher up than expected, and that the total energy they carried might not be sufficient, on its own, to heat the quiet corona.

143
00:12:50.660 --> 00:12:56.870
And here is the most maddening part of the whole business: the two suspects are not obliged to be rivals at all.

144
00:12:56.870 --> 00:13:06.920
When magnetic reconnection takes place, the field lines that snap and settle into a new configuration lash about like whips, and in doing so they generate precisely those waves.

145
00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:10.250
Nanoflares give birth to waves.

146
00:13:10.250 --> 00:13:17.100
Waves, in their turn, can collapse into turbulence, create small-scale current sheets, and trigger reconnection.

147
00:13:17.100 --> 00:13:22.350
Both suspects were at the scene of the crime, and in all likelihood they knew each other.

148
00:13:22.350 --> 00:13:26.640
Past a certain point, looking from a distance was no longer enough.

149
00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:30.130
Somebody had to go and measure it in place.

150
00:13:30.130 --> 00:13:36.180
On August twelfth, twenty eighteen, NASA launched a spacecraft it had named the Parker Solar Probe.

151
00:13:36.180 --> 00:13:47.670
The name belonged to Eugene Parker, the man who had predicted the solar wind theoretically and proposed the nanoflare idea; it was the first time NASA had named a mission after a living scientist.

152
00:13:47.670 --> 00:13:53.080
Parker was there in person that day, at ninety-one years old, to watch it go.

153
00:13:53.080 --> 00:13:56.570
The central design problem for the probe was survival.

154
00:13:56.570 --> 00:14:04.860
At its front sits a shield two point four metres across and about eleven centimetres thick, made of carbon foam pressed between two carbon plates.

155
00:14:04.860 --> 00:14:14.430
During the closest passes the front face of that shield climbs to one thousand four hundred degrees, while the instruments directly behind it carry on working at room temperature.

156
00:14:14.430 --> 00:14:20.320
The probe was programmed so that on each approach it would use the gravity of Venus to draw its orbit a little tighter.

157
00:14:20.320 --> 00:14:25.250
On April twenty-eighth, twenty twenty-one, something historic happened.

158
00:14:25.250 --> 00:14:32.420
At a distance of eighteen point eight solar radii, the probe crossed inside a boundary known as the Alfvén critical surface.

159
00:14:32.420 --> 00:14:44.230
That boundary defines where the solar atmosphere ends and the solar wind begins: inside it, the magnetic field rules the plasma and the particles are still bound to the Sun; outside it,

160
00:14:44.230 --> 00:14:47.400
the plasma drags the field along with it.

161
00:14:47.400 --> 00:14:51.530
The probe spent about five hours beneath that threshold.

162
00:14:51.530 --> 00:14:56.300
For the first time, humanity had touched the atmosphere of a star.

163
00:14:56.300 --> 00:14:59.150
Then it went closer still.

164
00:14:59.150 --> 00:15:10.160
On December twenty-fourth, twenty twenty-four, the probe passed within six point one million kilometres of the Sun's surface, travelling at six hundred ninety-two thousand kilometres per hour.

165
00:15:10.160 --> 00:15:15.890
The records for the closest and the fastest human-made object were broken in the same instant.

166
00:15:15.890 --> 00:15:21.140
For two days nobody knew whether the spacecraft had come through Christmas Eve alive.

167
00:15:21.140 --> 00:15:27.510
When the signal arrived on the evening of December twenty-sixth, the team learned that the probe was perfectly fine.

168
00:15:27.510 --> 00:15:30.040
So what was the answer?

169
00:15:30.040 --> 00:15:35.130
From its earliest approaches, the probe saw something nobody had expected.

170
00:15:35.130 --> 00:15:43.900
The magnetic field lines streaming away from the Sun, instead of running straight, were in places carving a sharp letter S and folding back on themselves.

171
00:15:43.900 --> 00:15:48.190
The field direction would flip in an instant, then flip back.

172
00:15:48.190 --> 00:15:53.120
These were named switchbacks, and they turned out to be widespread throughout the wind.

173
00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:58.370
A study published in twenty twenty-three traced that fingerprint back to its source.

174
00:15:58.370 --> 00:16:06.340
The distribution of the switchbacks the probe had measured matched the boundaries of the vast convection cells on the Sun's surface known as supergranules.

175
00:16:06.340 --> 00:16:17.030
Which is to say, the wind was not emerging from the surface as one smooth exhalation, but in intermittent clumps, out of funnel-shaped mouths at the cell edges where the magnetic field is concentrated.

176
00:16:17.030 --> 00:16:25.960
In the observations, the presence of high-energy ions and the shape of the energy distribution pointed to those clumps being produced by magnetic reconnection.

177
00:16:25.960 --> 00:16:31.850
What is more, the amount of energy released was on the scale required to drive the fast solar wind.

178
00:16:31.850 --> 00:16:35.340
That is powerful evidence for the nanoflare camp.

179
00:16:35.340 --> 00:16:45.230
Reconnection is no longer a theoretical possibility; it is a phenomenon measured in place, in the Sun's immediate neighbourhood, with an energy budget you can calculate.

180
00:16:45.230 --> 00:16:47.760
But the story did not end there.

181
00:16:47.760 --> 00:16:53.490
Data from the same probe also showed that the corona and the inner heliosphere are saturated with waves.

182
00:16:53.490 --> 00:17:01.780
The undulating fluctuations it measured were so pervasive and so energetic that they could account for much of the acceleration of the fast solar wind.

183
00:17:01.780 --> 00:17:07.030
In other words, the probe brought back evidence that both suspects are guilty.

184
00:17:07.030 --> 00:17:10.200
And that is exactly where matters stand today.

185
00:17:10.200 --> 00:17:16.410
For most of the twentieth century, the coronal heating problem was framed as the question of which one is right.

186
00:17:16.410 --> 00:17:20.860
The data of the twenty-first century has turned that into the wrong question.

187
00:17:20.860 --> 00:17:29.150
What is asked now is this: in which region of the solar atmosphere, in which magnetic structure, does which mechanism dominate?

188
00:17:29.150 --> 00:17:36.000
In the active regions, where field lines form closed arches, impulsive reconnection appears to take the lead.

189
00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:42.530
In coronal holes, where the field lines open out into space, wave turbulence is the stronger candidate.

190
00:17:42.530 --> 00:17:49.060
And the transition region, just above the chromosphere, may be a crossroads where both are at work at once.

191
00:17:49.060 --> 00:17:55.750
Searching for a single answer was rather like trying to explain the climate of an entire continent with one equation.

192
00:17:55.750 --> 00:17:59.560
It is worth adding that this matter does not stop at the Sun.

193
00:17:59.560 --> 00:18:03.530
The corona is not some quirk peculiar to our own star.

194
00:18:03.530 --> 00:18:13.740
Around every star with a magnetic field and convection we see hot, tenuous, X-ray-emitting atmospheres, and around some of them the emission is thousands of times brighter than the Sun's.

195
00:18:13.740 --> 00:18:22.030
The ferocious coronae of young, rapidly rotating stars are powerful enough to strip the atmospheres clean off the newborn planets around them.

196
00:18:22.030 --> 00:18:32.880
So understanding the mechanism that heats the corona does not merely satisfy an astrophysical curiosity; it is part of working out which stars can host planets capable of holding on to their air.

197
00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:36.690
Closer to home, there is a directly practical stake.

198
00:18:36.690 --> 00:18:43.700
The processes that heat the corona are the same processes that accelerate the solar wind and hurl out great eruptions of plasma.

199
00:18:43.700 --> 00:18:51.030
When those eruptions reach the Earth, they disturb power grids, satellites, flight routes and positioning systems.

200
00:18:51.030 --> 00:18:57.080
A model that can calculate the heating of the corona is a model that can genuinely forecast space weather.

201
00:18:57.080 --> 00:19:05.050
At present our predictions can tell us hours in advance whether a storm is coming; how hard it will strike is still something we struggle to say.

202
00:19:05.050 --> 00:19:11.100
This is where the road that began with that green line seen over Iowa in eighteen sixty-nine has arrived.

203
00:19:11.100 --> 00:19:13.470
We thought we had found a new element.

204
00:19:13.470 --> 00:19:15.200
We had not.

205
00:19:15.200 --> 00:19:18.850
We had found an unfamiliar state of a familiar one.

206
00:19:18.850 --> 00:19:29.060
We have been hunting for the energy that makes that state possible for more than eighty years, and by now we know who is responsible; what remains is who does how much of it, and where.

207
00:19:29.060 --> 00:19:38.950
Most of science moves this way in any case: the mystery is not dissolved, it gives way to a sharper, better-defined, more answerable mystery.

208
00:19:38.950 --> 00:19:42.760
Nöron Science'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

209
00:19:42.760 --> 00:19:45.930
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
